---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { Prose } from '../components/Prose';

/**
 * Written 2026-08-07 from src/data/cv.ts, which is the only place the career
 * is recorded as data. Nothing here is a fact that file does not carry.
 *
 * Still linked from nowhere — not the nav, not the footer. That was deliberate
 * while it was a stub, and is now just a leftover: one entry in nav.ts brings
 * it back. Left alone rather than changed, because adding a nav item is a
 * decision about what the site is for, not a copy fix.
 *
 * Prose lives here rather than in src/copy/ because this page is three
 * paragraphs with no arrangement to separate from its writing. If it grows a
 * second section, move it — the rule in CLAUDE.md holds and this is the
 * exception that proves nothing.
 */
---
<Page
  title="About"
  standfirst="I build the thing that makes the thing — design systems that hold across brands and platforms, for teams that have to ship faster than they can agree."
>
  <Prose lead>
    <p>
      Twelve years across mobile products and the systems underneath them. Most
      recently five at Expedia Group on the design system serving around ninety
      brands, and before that seven at Hotels.com designing the app itself — the
      home screen, the search results, the flows people actually book through. I
      have been on both sides of a design system: the team that maintains one,
      and the team that has to ship against one on a deadline.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two disciplines are the same problem at different altitudes. A product
      screen fails when it cannot answer a question about what a customer needs.
      A design system fails when it cannot answer a question about what a
      component is. Both get fixed the same way — stop shipping arrangements,
      start shipping something structured enough that a person, a team or a
      machine can build from it without having to ask. That is the argument both
      case studies here make, seven years apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      I am in London, looking for a lead role where the system and the product
      are one conversation rather than two teams negotiating.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Page>